{This is a Spiral Raise auction: after opener supports responder's major, responder asks with the cheapest step and opener climbs a coded ladder that pinpoints support length, strength, and shortness.
[BID 1C]
You hold a sound opening hand with no five-card major, so you start with a minor. 1\C is your better minor and keeps the auction low.
[BID 1H]
Partner responds 1\H, showing four or more hearts and enough to answer at the one level. A major fit may be waiting.
[BID 2H]
You raise to 2\H, agreeing hearts. By agreement this shows three- or four-card support, and with only three you also hold a ruffing value, so the raise is sound.
[BID 2S]
Partner asks with 2\S, the cheapest step. This is the Spiral ask: partner wants to know exactly how many trumps you have, how strong you are, and whether you are short somewhere.
[BID 3H]
You answer 3\H. On the ladder this shows three-card support with shortness in the other major, spades, a singleton or void there. Partner now knows both your trumps and your ruffing value.
[BID 4H]
Partner places the contract in 4\H. Knowing you have three trumps and a spade shortage, game in the 4-3 heart fit rates to play well; 3NT was a reasonable alternative, so this is a genuine judgment call.
[show NESW]
Your hand was \SJ \HQT6 \DQ982 \CAKQ42, a minimum minor opening with three-card heart support and a singleton spade. That singleton was the ruffing value that made the three-card raise sound, and 3\H told partner exactly where your shortness lay. With that picture partner chose the heart game over notrump, trusting the ruffing power of the 4-3 fit.}
