{Opener does not always raise. When the hand is flat with no ruffing value, opener rebids naturally instead of starting the Spiral, and the partnership finds its game the ordinary way.
[BID 1C]
With an opening hand and no five-card major, you start with a minor, 1\C.
[BID 1S]
Partner responds 1\S, showing four or more spades and enough to answer.
[BID 1N]
You rebid 1NT. With a balanced minimum and only three-card spade support without a ruffing value, you decline the raise and describe your shape and strength instead.
[BID 2N]
Partner invites game with 2NT, showing a balanced invitational hand and asking you to choose.
[BID 3S]
You bid 3\S, accepting the invitation and now showing your three-card spade support. This lets partner pick the right game, the 4-3 spade fit or notrump.
[BID 3N]
Partner chooses 3NT. Without an eight-card spade fit, notrump is the safer game.
[show NESW]
Your hand was \SA98 \H9532 \DKJ7 \CAQ4, a flat 3-4-3-3 maximum with no shortness. That is exactly the shape that declines the Spiral raise: three-card support but no ruffing value, so you rebid 1NT. When partner invited, your maximum accepted game, and showing the three spades let partner settle in the notrump game the 4-3 fit called for.}
